    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Warsaw grew from a fishing village into the seat of the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, and became the capital of the Polish Crown and the Commonwealth when Sigismund III moved his court from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> in 1596.</li><li>After the partitions it was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, ruled as the Duchy of Warsaw and Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, until it became the capital of independent Poland on 11 November 1918.</li><li>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> its Jewish population was confined in a ghetto and two uprisings were crushed, and about 85% of the city was destroyed; the Old Town was rebuilt after the war.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1647 Valerianus Magnus performed a vacuum experiment in Warsaw, repeating Torricelli's, which contradicted official European science.</p></aside>
    </section>

